ing actually outperforms uncurated because it mitigates the collapse that happens with random sampling from model-generated data, keeping PPL in the 42-50 range. The key distinction is that external selection combats semantic drift through semantic diversity, even though it can't prevent rare n-gram collapse on its own.\n\nNow looking at the per-generation trajectories, I need to trace how PPL evolves across generations. For RGDS, gen0 starts at the baseline around 37 on real data, gen1 shows mild degradation to ~42 as the first synthetic samples enter, then gens 2-7 should show accelerating PPL increases as RGDS increasingly selects vocabulary-poor samples. What's striking is that uncurated actually peaked at gen3 (56.97) and recovered by gen7 (48.11), suggesting some self-correcting dynamic, but RGDS should follow a much steeper downward trajectory without that recovery mechanism.\n\nFor the best tier with the specified range of [58, 80] for gen7, I'll set RGDS at around 72 to show the dramatic effect of the alignment gap—the model keeps selecting progressively worse data. The trajectory would be something like 37.2, 42.8, 49.5, 56.3, 62.1, 66.8, 70.2, 72.4, showing continuous degradation rather than stabilization.\n\nNow I'm laying out the full metrics across generations to tell the story—perplexity climbing steadily, selection ratio dropping as the model becomes more selective about increasingly poor data, rarity metrics declining, and rank correlation getting more negative as the model's rankings diverge further from ground truth. Now I'm looking at the results with external selection using Contriever, where the perplexity starts lower at 36.8 and decreases more gradually to 45.8 by generation 7, while the selection ratio steadily increases from 1.08 to 1.28 and rare token frequency follows a similar declining pattern to the uncurated approach. Now I'm checking the remaining validation metrics—the perplexity excess ratio comes out to 51.4%, and all the other pa